Say in the Brief that a plan is waiting for a decision

The Brief (attention queue) now describes a plan a board user asked for honestly. In questionRows, a pending card whose first interaction was created by a person and not by an agent is treated as asked by a person: its title falls back to 'Plan waiting for your decision' when the card has no title or summary (a titled card keeps its title, so the planner's 'Plan for: ...' shows as before), its blocking state is 'waiting' rather than 'stopped', and its consequence reads 'No tasks are created until you accept it. Waiting costs nothing.' (the request issue itself does already exist, so the promise is about the tasks in the plan, not about the request) Before this, the accept-only wake policy on such a card made the row claim an agent was paused on the issue, which is untrue for a board-created plan on an unassigned backlog container. Everything else about the row (key question:<issueId>, kind question, count, deep link to #interaction-<id>, lapse note) is unchanged, so the Inbox filter and badge counts keep working, and agent-created cards keep their existing 'The agent is paused on this issue until you answer' wording. A new embedded Postgres test file pins the person-asked row, the titled variant, the unchanged agent-created row, and that rejecting the plan (which cancels the container) removes the row. Not touched: a pre-existing em dash in a code comment at attention-queue.ts:485 (email handoff threshold note) belongs to earlier work and is not persisted or on-screen text.

